# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit the real .env.
#
# RPC_URL accepts either an https:// or a wss:// endpoint. wss:// is
# recommended when WATCH_MODE=true -- block events arrive as a push over
# the open socket instead of via HTTPS polling, a real latency win
# (confirmed live against Alchemy's testnet endpoints, same API key, just
# swap the scheme: wss://<host>/v2/<key> instead of https://...). Plain
# https:// still works fine for one-shot (non-WATCH_MODE) runs.
RPC_URL=
PRIVATE_KEY=
EXECUTOR_ADDRESS=

# Opportunity-discovery scan config -- see notes/03 - Address Registry.md
# for which adapter/token addresses are actually confirmed live+approved
# on a given network. ADAPTERS, BORROWED_ASSET, and VIA_TOKENS are all
# required for main() to scan anything.
#
# ADAPTERS: comma-separated `address:protocol` list of DEX adapters to
#   route through -- protocol is one of v2, v3, curve, balancer, and
#   defaults to v3 if omitted (matching this project's original
#   single-adapter config, e.g. `0xAbC...` alone still works unchanged).
#   Getting the protocol tag right matters: it controls how routeData's
#   `extra` field gets encoded, and each protocol expects a different
#   shape -- see the adapter contracts themselves (UniswapV2Adapter.sol,
#   UniswapV3Adapter.sol, CurveAdapter.sol, BalancerAdapter.sol) for the
#   exact encoding each one decodes on-chain.
#     - v2/v3 are token-agnostic: every adapter1 x adapter2 combination
#       among your v2/v3 adapters gets scanned against every VIA_TOKENS
#       entry automatically.
#     - curve: the adapter's own pool is queried on-chain at startup
#       (coins(0)/coins(1)) to discover which token pair it actually
#       trades -- no via-token search needed or possible, since one
#       Curve adapter instance is bound to one already-deployed pool.
#     - balancer: encoding is implemented (see encodeBalancerRouteData in
#       index.js) but candidate generation isn't wired up yet -- a
#       poolId isn't derivable from just the vault/token addresses the
#       way Curve's coin indices are, and there's no real Balancer pool
#       verified live on either testnet yet to design that against. See
#       the comment above buildCandidates() in index.js for what adding
#       one would need.
#   Example: `0xRouter02Adapter:v3,0xSushiV2Adapter:v2,0x3poolAdapter:curve`
# BORROWED_ASSET: the token main() borrows and must return to (e.g. WETH).
#   Its on-chain decimals() is read automatically at startup -- SCAN_AMOUNT
#   and MIN_PROFIT below are parsed in *this* token's units, not always 18
#   (e.g. USDC is 6). The WETH-only gas-cost adjustment (see tryRoute() in
#   index.js) only applies when this is actually the network's WETH.
# VIA_TOKENS: comma-separated list of other approved tokens to route
#   through, e.g. USDC,WSOL addresses on Ethereum Sepolia. With 2 or more
#   configured, triangular routes (BORROWED_ASSET -> viaA -> viaB ->
#   BORROWED_ASSET) are scanned automatically alongside the plain 2-hop
#   round trips, for v2/v3 adapters -- see buildCandidates() in index.js.
ADAPTERS=
BORROWED_ASSET=
VIA_TOKENS=

# Optional scan tuning -- all have defaults in index.js if left blank.
# FEE_TIERS: comma-separated Uniswap V3 fee tiers to try per hop (default: 500,3000,10000). v2/curve legs ignore this entirely.
# SCAN_AMOUNT: amount of BORROWED_ASSET to test per candidate, in BORROWED_ASSET's own decimal units (default: 1)
# MIN_PROFIT: minimum profit to submit a request, in BORROWED_ASSET's own decimal units (default: 0.01)
# SLIPPAGE_BPS: max slippage per leg in basis points (default: 300)
# SCAN_CONCURRENCY: how many quoteRoute() calls to fire in parallel per
#   batch (default: 20) -- candidate count grows combinatorially with
#   ADAPTERS x VIA_TOKENS x FEE_TIERS, so this caps how hard a broad scan
#   hits your RPC provider at once.
# WATCH_MODE: set to "true" to re-scan on every new block instead of
#   running once and exiting (Ctrl+C to stop). Uses a wss:// RPC_URL if
#   you've set one (see above); falls back to HTTPS polling otherwise.
# CONFIRMATION_TIMEOUT_MS: how long to wait for a submitted tx to confirm
#   before treating it as stuck and freeing its nonce with a cancellation
#   tx (default: 120000 = 2 minutes). Also bounds the cancellation tx's
#   own confirmation wait, so a relay that won't even confirm a bare
#   self-transfer can't block this process forever either.
# ALLOW_UNKNOWN_SUBMIT_RELAY: set to "true" to allow a SUBMIT_RPC_URL host
#   not on the known-private-relay allowlist in index.js (rpc.flashbots.net,
#   rpc.mevblocker.io, mainnet.base.org) on a mainnet chain. Off by default
#   so a typo'd or misconfigured relay fails loudly instead of silently
#   submitting with no MEV protection.
FEE_TIERS=
SCAN_AMOUNT=
MIN_PROFIT=
SLIPPAGE_BPS=
SCAN_CONCURRENCY=
WATCH_MODE=
CONFIRMATION_TIMEOUT_MS=
ALLOW_UNKNOWN_SUBMIT_RELAY=

# Local reserve-math pre-filter (see prefilterCandidates() in index.js) --
# optional. Both blank (the default) disables it entirely: every candidate
# still reaches the real quoteRoute() check exactly as before this existed.
# Set whichever factory address(es) match the protocols in ADAPTERS above
# to enable it for that protocol -- v3-only, v2-only, or both work fine.
# V3_FACTORY_ADDRESS: Uniswap V3 factory address on this network -- reads
#   slot0() per unique pool to rank candidates by rough spot-price
#   estimate before quoting (ignores concentrated-liquidity depth --
#   ranking signal only, not a substitute for quoteRoute()).
# V2_FACTORY_ADDRESS: Uniswap V2-style factory address on this network --
#   reads getReserves() per unique pool; this one IS the exact
#   constant-product formula, not an approximation.
# PREFILTER_TOP_N: how many top-ranked candidates survive the pre-filter
#   and go on to a real quoteRoute() call (default: 25). Only takes effect
#   once at least one factory address above is set, and only when there
#   are actually more candidates than this to trim.
# ADAPTER_FACTORY_OVERRIDES: "adapterAddress:factoryAddress,..." -- only
#   needed when two ADAPTERS entries share a protocol tag (e.g. two
#   different 'v2' DEXes on the same network) but trade through different
#   factories. V2_FACTORY_ADDRESS/V3_FACTORY_ADDRESS above still cover
#   every adapter of that protocol NOT listed here; this is strictly an
#   override for the ones that need a different factory. Without this,
#   two same-protocol adapters trading the identical pair would silently
#   share one cached price between them in the pre-filter/trade sizer --
#   real quoteRoute() submission decisions are unaffected either way
#   (this only ever influences ranking/sizing, never fund safety), but
#   set this whenever it applies so ranking/sizing is actually accurate.
V3_FACTORY_ADDRESS=
V2_FACTORY_ADDRESS=
ADAPTER_FACTORY_OVERRIDES=
PREFILTER_TOP_N=

# Trade sizer -- optional, only takes effect once the pre-filter above is
# enabled (reuses its price cache, zero extra RPC calls). Instead of
# always testing SCAN_AMOUNT, finds the amount within
# [TRADE_SIZER_MIN_AMOUNT, TRADE_SIZER_MAX_AMOUNT] that locally maximizes
# each surviving candidate's round-trip output (see
# findOptimalTradeSize() in lib.js). Both in BORROWED_ASSET's own decimal
# units, same as SCAN_AMOUNT -- default to 1% and 1000x of SCAN_AMOUNT if
# left blank. Deliberately NOT bounded by maxLoanAmount -- that's still
# enforced downstream before any real submission, but bounding the
# search by it here would make sizing pointless during DRY_RUN, where
# maxLoanAmount is deliberately 0.
TRADE_SIZER_MIN_AMOUNT=
TRADE_SIZER_MAX_AMOUNT=

# NETWORK_LABEL: human-readable tag for whatever network RPC_URL points
#   at (e.g. "Base Sepolia") -- stamped on every row in shared/data/bot.db
#   and every log line, and scopes the circuit breaker's counters per
#   network. No safe default -- an unlabeled run's DB rows are much less
#   useful later, so this is worth setting even though nothing breaks if
#   you don't.
# DRY_RUN: set to "true" to run every real on-chain check (quoteRoute,
#   gas estimate) exactly as normal, but stop short of the actual
#   requestFlashLoanArbitrage() call -- logs and records what WOULD have
#   been submitted, for zero gas and zero risk. Good for running
#   unattended over a long stretch to see whether real opportunities
#   exist before funding anything.
NETWORK_LABEL=
DRY_RUN=

# Off-chain circuit breaker -- see tripCircuitBreakerIfNeeded() in
# index.js. A consecutive-loss/consecutive-revert breaker is
# structurally impossible on-chain (a reverted transaction leaves no
# trace for a future call to read), so it lives here instead, backed by
# shared/data/bot.db. Once tripped, tryRoute() returns immediately for
# every candidate without touching the chain at all, until manually
# resumed (no auto-reset -- see shared/db.js's resumeCircuitBreaker()).
# MAX_CONSECUTIVE_FAILURES: trip after this many back-to-back reverted/
#   failed submissions on this network (default: 5).
# MAX_DAILY_GAS_SPEND_ETH: trip once today's total confirmed/submitted
#   gas cost on this network reaches this many ETH. Unset (0) by
#   default -- deliberately opt-in, since a meaningful figure depends on
#   your own risk tolerance and this network's real value, not something
#   safe to default (same reasoning as the contract's own maxLoanAmount
#   starting at 0).
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL: optional Slack/Discord-compatible incoming-webhook
#   URL -- POSTed the moment the circuit breaker actually trips, so you
#   find out immediately rather than next time you happen to check logs.
#   Separate from monitoring/monitor.js's own webhook -- this fires from
#   inside a submission attempt, that one from watching chain events.
MAX_CONSECUTIVE_FAILURES=
MAX_DAILY_GAS_SPEND_ETH=
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# LOG_LEVEL: debug/info/warn/error (default: info) -- see shared/logger.js.
# LOG_DIR: where dated log files get written (default: shared/logs/,
#   next to shared/data/bot.db). Console output is unaffected either way.
LOG_LEVEL=
LOG_DIR=

# Request timing/risk knobs -- previously hardcoded in tryRoute(),
# externalized since they're genuine tradeoffs (network congestion,
# staleness tolerance, gas-backstop conservatism) rather than mechanical
# implementation details. All optional, default to what was hardcoded
# before.
# EXECUTE_BEFORE_SECONDS: how long a submitted request has to land
#   before it reverts as expired (default: 120 = 2 minutes).
# DEADLINE_SECONDS: per-adapter router-call deadline window (default:
#   300 = 5 minutes).
# GAS_HEADROOM_PCT: percentage applied to the gas-adjusted profit floor
#   (default: 125 = 25% headroom) -- see tryRoute()'s comment for
#   exactly what blind spots this covers.
EXECUTE_BEFORE_SECONDS=
DEADLINE_SECONDS=
GAS_HEADROOM_PCT=

# Where requestFlashLoanArbitrage actually gets submitted -- separate from
# RPC_URL (used for all read-only calls) so submission can go through a
# private relay instead of the public mempool. Falls back to RPC_URL when
# left blank, so testnets work with no extra config.
#
# Network-dependent -- this is NOT the same endpoint on every chain:
#   - Ethereum mainnet: a private relay, e.g. Flashbots Protect
#     (https://rpc.flashbots.net) or MEV Blocker (https://rpc.mevblocker.io),
#     so the tx never sits in the public mempool for a searcher to copy.
#   - Base mainnet: Base runs a single sequencer, not a public-mempool-plus-
#     builder-auction model, so the private-relay concept above doesn't
#     apply the same way -- point this at Base's own official RPC instead.
#     Verify current best practice for this closer to actual mainnet launch
#     rather than assuming it hasn't changed.
#   - Testnets (Sepolia, Base Sepolia): leave blank, falls back to RPC_URL.
SUBMIT_RPC_URL=
